# The browsers this app is built for — and the reason it has to say so (#429).
#
# Tailwind v4 ships its palette as `oklch()`. Next 16's CSS optimiser rewrites
# that to `lab()` for browsers that support `lab` but not `oklch`, and because
# the `lab()` declaration comes last, EVERY browser gets the degraded colour —
# including the ones that support `oklch` perfectly well.
#
# The conversion is not exact, and the app could not afford the difference.
# `text-slate-500` is the muted-text idiom here, 130 call sites across 48 files:
#
#                       renders as   on the #f8fafc ground   on white
#   Tailwind's fallback  #62748e            4.55 ✅            4.76 ✅
#   the lab() Next emits #677992            4.24 ❌            4.44 ❌
#
# So after the Next 16 upgrade every secondary line in the app was below WCAG
# AA, and the accessibility gate caught it on /admin (13 nodes) and /catalog.
# Before the upgrade the built stylesheet had 58 `oklch()` and no `lab()`.
#
# Pinning the targets stops the rewrite: the build emits `oklch()` and the
# palette renders as it was designed and measured.
#
# This is NOT a new browser-support decision. These are Tailwind CSS v4's own
# documented minimums, and the app already cannot run below them — Tailwind v4
# needs `@property`, cascade layers and `color-mix()`. The `lab()` fallback was
# charged to every modern browser and bought nothing: anything old enough to
# need it is already broken by Tailwind itself.
chrome >= 111
edge >= 111
firefox >= 128
safari >= 16.4
